WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.560
The first film used ordered arrival to map a chromosome.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.640
How can a phage reveal which genes sit close together?

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.580
Keep following gold donor DNA.

4
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.880
The carrier has changed, so the readout must change too.

5
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:25.453
Genes can cross a cell-blocking filter inside phage particles, protected from DNase.

6
00:00:26.633 --> 00:00:32.393
In generalized transduction, a phage sometimes packages host DNA instead of phage

7
00:00:32.393 --> 00:00:35.773
DNA. Any host region can be sampled.

8
00:00:36.993 --> 00:00:41.153
The coat reveals the route; the host fragment creates the mapping signal.

9
00:00:42.183 --> 00:00:45.863
P22 carries host fragments in Salmonella.

10
00:00:47.163 --> 00:00:48.423
P1 does this in E. coli.

11
00:00:49.823 --> 00:00:54.103
Each transducing particle delivers only a limited chromosome segment.

12
00:00:55.423 --> 00:00:59.103
The phage carries a short window, not the whole circular chromosome.

13
00:01:00.317 --> 00:01:03.357
Specialized transduction follows another rule.

14
00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:08.037
Lambda inserts at one preferred site between gal and bio.

15
00:01:09.417 --> 00:01:12.557
Inaccurate removal can carry only neighbouring host genes.

16
00:01:13.857 --> 00:01:18.877
This is not random sampling across the chromosome; attachment-site position matters.

17
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:22.297
Return to generalized transduction.

18
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:28.057
Two genes can share one particle only when one host fragment covers both.

19
00:01:29.477 --> 00:01:32.457
Closer markers fit together in more possible windows.

20
00:01:33.757 --> 00:01:37.397
Move the window and count how often both markers remain inside.

21
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:41.617
Two marker pairs produce different shared-carriage bars.

22
00:01:42.957 --> 00:01:44.917
One bar is high, and one is low.

23
00:01:46.217 --> 00:01:47.917
Which pair lies closer on the chromosome?

24
00:01:49.437 --> 00:01:52.277
Compare only the bars; do not infer direction yet.

25
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.040
The pair with the higher shared-carriage bar is closer.

26
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:07.080
More fragment positions can include both markers, so more particles deliver them

27
00:02:07.080 --> 00:02:12.980
together. The bar summarizes many particles, not one specially chosen fragment.

28
00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.840
Researchers select recipients that gained one donor marker.

29
00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.720
Then they score the fraction that also gained another.

30
00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:25.740
That fraction is the cotransduction frequency.

31
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.220
Selection defines the denominator before the second marker is counted.

32
00:02:32.333 --> 00:02:37.953
Frequencies against one selected marker rank proximity, but cannot place genes left

33
00:02:37.953 --> 00:02:41.993
or right. Three-factor classes settle the order.

34
00:02:43.253 --> 00:02:46.953
The same proximity can sit on either side of the selected marker.

35
00:02:48.117 --> 00:02:50.317
Shared carriage is not the whole colony.

36
00:02:51.617 --> 00:02:54.357
The donor fragment must enter and be expressed.

37
00:02:55.637 --> 00:03:00.337
For stable inheritance, it must also recombine into the recipient chromosome.

38
00:03:01.777 --> 00:03:06.937
A colony combines transport, recombination, expression, and survival under

39
00:03:06.937 --> 00:03:07.937
selection.

40
00:03:08.467 --> 00:03:12.467
Interrupted mating maps long chromosome stretches by time.

41
00:03:13.847 --> 00:03:17.307
Cotransduction resolves a local interval by shared carriage.

42
00:03:18.667 --> 00:03:20.667
Each route creates its own map readout.

43
00:03:21.927 --> 00:03:25.547
Neither readout gives physical base pairs directly in this lesson.

44
00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:30.347
For generalized transduction, shared carriage maps proximity.

45
00:03:31.707 --> 00:03:36.087
Closer genes travel together more often, within the phage's limited load.

46
00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:41.187
Compare markers under the same phage and experimental conditions.
